            <ArticleTitle>Trunk stabilisation exercises reduce sternal separation in chronic sternal instability after cardiac surgery: a randomised cross-over trial.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>255-60</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="QUESTION" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Do trunk stabilisation exercises reduce sternal separation and pain, and improve the quality and control of the performance of tasks in individuals with chronic sternal instability?</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Randomised crossover study with concealed allocation and intention-to-treat analysis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Nine individuals with chronic sternal instability following a median sternotomy for cardiac surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">The experimental intervention consisted of six weeks of trunk stabilisation exercises; the control intervention was no exercises.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Outcomes were sternal separation measured by ultrasound in mm, pain during the performance of nine everyday tasks measured on a 100-mm visual analogue scale, and the quality and control of the performance of two tasks scored on a 100-mm visual analogue scale.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Overall, sternal separation during the period of trunk stabilisation exercises decreased by 6.2 mm (95% CI 3.5 to 8.9) more than during the control period. Overall, pain decreased when performing everyday tasks by 14 mm (95% CI 5 to 23) more than during the control period. Overall, task performance during the period of trunk stabilisation exercises did not improve (mean difference 10 mm, 95% CI -3 to 22) more than during the control period.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Trunk stabilisation exercises should be included in the rehabilitation of individuals who experience sternal instability following cardiac surgery. A larger trial is warranted to determine if stabilisation exercises are beneficial in improving the quality and control of task performance.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
